(swoosh)

00:01:05.628 --> 00:01:09.640
- Have you ever heard
that advice to take risks

00:01:09.640 --> 00:01:12.023
but you felt like, well I
don't want to take risks,

00:01:12.023 --> 00:01:13.439
no one likes risks, right?

00:01:13.439 --> 00:01:15.655
No one wants to say when
they wake up in the morning,

00:01:15.655 --> 00:01:17.518
"I'm going to take tons of risks today."

00:01:17.518 --> 00:01:20.164
Usually risks brings about fear.

00:01:20.164 --> 00:01:22.809
We're worried that if we
take a risk, we'll fail,

00:01:22.809 --> 00:01:25.869
other people will judge
us, we'll look stupid,

00:01:25.869 --> 00:01:28.741
we'll feel uncertained
or unsure of ourselves

00:01:28.741 --> 00:01:31.281
and nobody likes those
feelings or those outcomes.

00:01:31.281 --> 00:01:35.488
So as much as people say take
risks, it's easy for us to say

00:01:35.488 --> 00:01:37.134
"Nah, I'm not into that."

00:01:37.134 --> 00:01:41.198
But we also know intellectually
that we can't grow

00:01:41.198 --> 00:01:43.467
unless we take more risks.

00:01:43.467 --> 00:01:47.424
That your next level of success,
that leap from this level

00:01:47.424 --> 00:01:49.721
to that level, there's
going to be risks there.

00:01:49.721 --> 00:01:50.994
You know, it's from here to there

00:01:50.994 --> 00:01:52.742
and there's that gap in between.

00:01:52.742 --> 00:01:55.145
You don't know as you take that leap

00:01:55.145 --> 00:01:56.824
if you're going to do
well, that's the risk.

00:01:56.824 --> 00:01:59.707
You might fall, you might
fail, you might not do well

00:01:59.707 --> 00:02:02.929
but if you're not making those
leaps and taking those steps,

00:02:02.929 --> 00:02:05.743
you'll always be stuck
where you were yesterday.

00:02:05.743 --> 00:02:08.927
And to grow, we've got
to have a lot of courage

00:02:08.927 --> 00:02:10.891
to jump into those risks.

00:02:10.891 --> 00:02:13.893
(light jingle)

00:02:13.893 --> 00:02:14.726
(swoosh)

00:02:14.726 --> 00:02:18.059
- New businesses start out small and so,

00:02:19.132 --> 00:02:22.329
it would be very easy for
say, the person who runs

00:02:22.329 --> 00:02:25.506
our US books category, to say

00:02:25.506 --> 00:02:30.110
"Why are we doing these
experiments with things

00:02:30.110 --> 00:02:34.110
"that generated a tiny
bit of revenue last year?

00:02:35.015 --> 00:02:39.590
"Why don't we instead focus
those resources and that,

00:02:39.590 --> 00:02:44.136
"all that brain power
on the books category

00:02:44.136 --> 00:02:46.232
"which is a big business for us."

00:02:46.232 --> 00:02:50.086
And instead, that would be a
natural thing to have happen.

00:02:50.086 --> 00:02:54.302
But instead, in side Amazon,
when a new business reaches

00:02:54.302 --> 00:02:57.601
some small milestone of sales,

00:02:57.601 --> 00:02:59.292
email messages go around

00:02:59.292 --> 00:03:01.803
and everybody's giving virtual high fives

00:03:01.803 --> 00:03:03.979
for reaching that milestone.

00:03:03.979 --> 00:03:07.922
And I think it's because we
know from our past experiences

00:03:07.922 --> 00:03:10.255
that big things start small.

00:03:11.464 --> 00:03:13.610
The biggest oak starts from an acorn

00:03:13.610 --> 00:03:15.301
and you've got to reckon...

00:03:15.301 --> 00:03:18.844
If you want to do anything
new, you've got to be willing

00:03:18.844 --> 00:03:21.146
to let that acorn grow
into a little sappling

00:03:21.146 --> 00:03:23.988
and then finally into a
small tree and maybe one day

00:03:23.988 --> 00:03:27.253
it'll be a big business on its own.

00:03:27.253 --> 00:03:30.104
- Another guy interviews,
a man named Kyle Bass.

00:03:30.104 --> 00:03:32.851
Kyle Bass took $30,000,000,
gathered money together,

00:03:32.851 --> 00:03:34.649
a lot of money but other people's money,

00:03:34.649 --> 00:03:36.792
turned it into 2,000,000,000
in the middle of

00:03:36.792 --> 00:03:38.346
the Subprime crisis.

00:03:38.346 --> 00:03:39.495
When the whole world
- That's a very

00:03:39.495 --> 00:03:40.362
- is blowing up.
- big return.

00:03:40.362 --> 00:03:41.195
(Tony laughs)

00:03:41.195 --> 00:03:42.428
- You could say that to the ears right?

00:03:42.428 --> 00:03:43.286
- [Man Interviewing Tony] Oh my God.

00:03:43.286 --> 00:03:45.722
- So I'm like how did you
do this, it's historic.

00:03:45.722 --> 00:03:47.384
If there was several books
you read and they open up

00:03:47.384 --> 00:03:50.748
the chapter in some of the
most historic books recently,

00:03:50.748 --> 00:03:52.919
the gentlemen who wrote,
Fly Boy's also wrote

00:03:52.919 --> 00:03:54.205
one of the books and
starts out talking about

00:03:54.205 --> 00:03:56.420
Kyle Bass doing this,
this incredible piece.

00:03:56.420 --> 00:03:58.565
It's like being there with him.

00:03:58.565 --> 00:04:01.155
But the way he did it
was, he risked six cents

00:04:01.155 --> 00:04:03.885
to make a dollar, and you
just leverage that up.

00:04:03.885 --> 00:04:07.851
So he could be wrong how many
times and still break even?

00:04:07.851 --> 00:04:11.200
He could be wrong 15 times and make money.

00:04:11.200 --> 00:04:13.411
So what, he's also smart as hell.

00:04:13.411 --> 00:04:14.802
He looked at things, he evaluated things,

00:04:14.802 --> 00:04:16.245
it's more detailed that
I explained in the book

00:04:16.245 --> 00:04:18.289
but the core they all have in common is

00:04:18.289 --> 00:04:20.847
they risk for valuable returns.

00:04:20.847 --> 00:04:21.843
(stomp)
- I want to make whatever

00:04:21.843 --> 00:04:24.519
I'm doing harder.

00:04:24.519 --> 00:04:25.885
I know that sounds weird but I'm like,

00:04:25.885 --> 00:04:29.291
how could I make my job more difficult?

00:04:29.291 --> 00:04:31.520
Because in taking that risk to make it

00:04:31.520 --> 00:04:33.433
more difficult, I'll grow.

00:04:33.433 --> 00:04:35.231
I'll give you guys an example.

00:04:35.231 --> 00:04:38.680
Last weekend, I had
the blessing to keynote

00:04:38.680 --> 00:04:41.419
a conference in New Orleans,
some of you all were there.

00:04:41.419 --> 00:04:44.520
It was at the Superdome in
New Orleans which holds like

00:04:44.520 --> 00:04:47.890
100,000 people but they
kind of spliced it in half

00:04:47.890 --> 00:04:49.857
with a curtain and they had 28,

00:04:49.857 --> 00:04:51.964
30,000 people in the audience.

00:04:51.964 --> 00:04:55.472
This was for the Beachbody
convention and it was a blast.

00:04:55.472 --> 00:04:58.111
Now I could get up and
talk to 20,000 people

00:04:58.111 --> 00:04:59.748
pretty regularly in my
life, I'm doing it again

00:04:59.748 --> 00:05:02.062
next weekend, it's what I
do as a motivational speaker

00:05:02.062 --> 00:05:03.977
right, I'm used to doing that.

00:05:03.977 --> 00:05:07.136
But I could just go on stage
and do my thing and get off

00:05:07.136 --> 00:05:10.935
and that wouldn't be serving
the audience as much.

00:05:10.935 --> 00:05:14.411
And so I ask myself, how
can I make this presentation

00:05:14.411 --> 00:05:17.459
even harder on me so that I can grow?

00:05:17.459 --> 00:05:19.915
So I texted two of my
friends who happened to be

00:05:19.915 --> 00:05:22.218
in that organization, Tony
Horton, many of you guys

00:05:22.218 --> 00:05:26.327
know he is kind of the
founder and trainer for P90X,

00:05:26.327 --> 00:05:28.480
and Chalene Johnson's who does PiYo.

00:05:28.480 --> 00:05:31.042
And I texted them both, I
said, "What's your hardest

00:05:31.042 --> 00:05:34.923
"exercise move that
people usually can't get?"

00:05:34.923 --> 00:05:36.556
Cuz both those people are
going to be at the conference

00:05:36.556 --> 00:05:38.408
and this was a lot of their super fans

00:05:38.408 --> 00:05:40.241
so I was like let's go.

00:05:40.241 --> 00:05:42.047
So Tony told me, he's like,

00:05:42.047 --> 00:05:44.032
"The donkey kick is the hardest one."

00:05:44.032 --> 00:05:45.995
The donkey kick is
basically you do handstand,

00:05:45.995 --> 00:05:48.652
you kick your legs out, you
land back plank position,

00:05:48.652 --> 00:05:52.052
you run your leg through like
threading the needle in yoga,

00:05:52.052 --> 00:05:54.774
and then you return and
you do a burpee, okay.

00:05:54.774 --> 00:05:57.878
And Chalene said, "Well
the hardest one is like

00:05:57.878 --> 00:05:59.974
"which cause the Piyo flip," which is like

00:05:59.974 --> 00:06:02.861
imagine doing a bridge,
right, but then going

00:06:02.861 --> 00:06:05.667
to kind of one arm and it's
like you're doing a bridge

00:06:05.667 --> 00:06:06.944
this way and one arm goes that way.

00:06:06.944 --> 00:06:08.220
I mean, it's not easy.

00:06:08.220 --> 00:06:11.926
So I decided in front of 20,000 people

00:06:11.926 --> 00:06:16.630
to do those two moves
back to back in a suit.

00:06:16.630 --> 00:06:18.500
So imagine doing a handstand in the suit,

00:06:18.500 --> 00:06:20.332
kicking your legs out,
threading the needle,

00:06:20.332 --> 00:06:22.937
then flipping over doing
a full extended bridge.

00:06:22.937 --> 00:06:27.567
Not easy, but I did it
because it would be hard.

00:06:27.567 --> 00:06:28.945
Does that make sense?

00:06:28.945 --> 00:06:32.237
Part of the reason I got
better as a speaker is because

00:06:32.237 --> 00:06:34.570
I was pushing myself, pushing myself.

00:06:34.570 --> 00:06:36.179
I mean, I used to be...

00:06:36.179 --> 00:06:38.597
It's very comforting and
easy to give a big speech

00:06:38.597 --> 00:06:40.232
and you know, have a transcript

00:06:40.232 --> 00:06:42.032
or all these notes to read from.

00:06:42.032 --> 00:06:45.179
But I said, "One day I'm going
to be able to get up on stage

00:06:45.179 --> 00:06:48.544
"and give a speech with
no notes whatsoever."

00:06:48.544 --> 00:06:52.784
Now that's hard, that's not
comforting, it's not easy.

00:06:52.784 --> 00:06:54.299
But it's by putting challenges

00:06:54.299 --> 00:06:56.028
in front of ourselves that we grow.

00:06:56.028 --> 00:06:57.956
And that's part of taking risks.

00:06:57.956 --> 00:07:00.538
You know, I will say to people,
"Look if the only challenges

00:07:00.538 --> 00:07:03.144
"you're dealing with in life
right now are the ones that

00:07:03.144 --> 00:07:05.609
"life dealt you, then you're not

00:07:05.609 --> 00:07:08.007
"living a fully conscious life yet."

00:07:08.007 --> 00:07:11.237
And what I mean by that is
you're living a life of reaction.

00:07:11.237 --> 00:07:13.696
If you're not moving and put...

00:07:13.696 --> 00:07:15.615
If you're not moving
towards harder and more

00:07:15.615 --> 00:07:18.039
difficult goals, if you're not
giving yourself challenges,

00:07:18.039 --> 00:07:20.807
if you're not taking risks
consciously, then you're just

00:07:20.807 --> 00:07:23.028
reacting to the world, you're not growing.

00:07:23.028 --> 00:07:24.798
And so, challenge yourself.

00:07:24.798 --> 00:07:26.628
The next time you're
going to go do something,

00:07:26.628 --> 00:07:29.073
your art, your career,
put yourself out there,

00:07:29.073 --> 00:07:30.925
try something, make that sales call.

00:07:30.925 --> 00:07:32.950
How could you challenge
yourself to make that process

00:07:32.950 --> 00:07:35.434
even harder so you get better.

00:07:35.434 --> 00:07:39.287
- Both of us right, have given
up a lot for our careers.

00:07:39.287 --> 00:07:41.810
And being successful, and
most people don't like it

00:07:41.810 --> 00:07:44.228
when I say this, it has costs.

00:07:44.228 --> 00:07:46.270
We're not having children
or not having friends

00:07:46.270 --> 00:07:47.928
or not having a social life.

00:07:47.928 --> 00:07:49.833
And so at some point, you have to decide

00:07:49.833 --> 00:07:52.016
whether that cost is worth it or not.

00:07:52.016 --> 00:07:53.594
The question isn't
really whether you should

00:07:53.594 --> 00:07:55.736
take this risk really is it?

00:07:55.736 --> 00:07:59.461
It's what else do I really
want to do in addition to this.

00:07:59.461 --> 00:08:01.279
Am I right about that.
- I think so, yeah.

00:08:01.279 --> 00:08:03.160
- And I think maybe that's
how I view the risk is

00:08:03.160 --> 00:08:06.640
I'm not necessary risking
my company if I decide

00:08:06.640 --> 00:08:08.948
to push it further, I'm risking my life.

00:08:08.948 --> 00:08:11.554
- It's not just writing a
check, it's I now have to

00:08:11.554 --> 00:08:13.317
give like a part of my finger.

00:08:13.317 --> 00:08:14.150
- Yeah

00:08:14.150 --> 00:08:15.281
- Forever.
- Cause it's going to take

00:08:15.281 --> 00:08:16.408
more of your time.

00:08:16.408 --> 00:08:17.440
- Forever.
(beat music)

00:08:17.440 --> 00:08:19.254
And so that's the tradeoff.

00:08:19.254 --> 00:08:22.336
- I truly believe that
whatever you have as a skillset

00:08:22.336 --> 00:08:24.775
and if you want to start
a business, it cannot be

00:08:24.775 --> 00:08:27.240
outside of the horizon
on where you're at today

00:08:27.240 --> 00:08:28.268
and where you want to get to.

00:08:28.268 --> 00:08:31.056
So if you've absolutely
come into an industry

00:08:31.056 --> 00:08:33.781
and never been exposed
to it, talked to people

00:08:33.781 --> 00:08:35.799
in that industry, have any context for it,

00:08:35.799 --> 00:08:38.614
have any experience whatsoever,
that is kind of crazy.

00:08:38.614 --> 00:08:40.398
I really think it should be about you...

00:08:40.398 --> 00:08:43.294
The first leg of the business,
the first testing zone,

00:08:43.294 --> 00:08:46.363
the first revenue should
be about 20% further than

00:08:46.363 --> 00:08:48.108
where you have been in the past.

00:08:48.108 --> 00:08:49.616
So may you've done the
work, you've worked for

00:08:49.616 --> 00:08:50.843
another company and
now you want to do that

00:08:50.843 --> 00:08:51.978
as your own business.

00:08:51.978 --> 00:08:53.555
You've already got that part,

00:08:53.555 --> 00:08:55.896
the 20% is really starting the business.

00:08:55.896 --> 00:08:59.012
So I call it within the
horizon of where you're at

00:08:59.012 --> 00:09:00.273
and where you want to get to.

00:09:00.273 --> 00:09:02.152
And every time you get to that new level,

00:09:02.152 --> 00:09:05.299
the cool part is the horizon
gets further and further away

00:09:05.299 --> 00:09:06.914
and that's how growth happens.

